    "blogtextliquid_LBJgtB": {
      "type": "blogtextliquid",
      "custom_css": [
        "@media screen and (min-width: 1024px) {.shopify-hardcoded-text-section-content {font-size: 24px !important; margin: 0 !important; } .shopify-hardcoded-text-section-wrapper {margin-bottom: -85px; }}",
        ".shopify-hardcoded-text-section-content {font-size: 16px; margin-left: 0px; text-align: left;}",
        "@media screen and (max-width: 1024px) {.shopify-hardcoded-text-section-wrapper {min-height: 200px; padding: 16px !important; }}"
      ],
      "name": "Blog Text Liquid",
      "settings": {
        "text_content": "<p>You put it on in the morning, carefully, and by 11am it is gone. Not faded. Gone. You did everything right: let it dry naturally instead of rubbing it in (you read that tip somewhere and took it seriously). And still, by the time you are reaching for your second iced Starbucks, you might as well be wearing nothing. Just the memory of something that smelled great a few hours ago, before the day got going. This happens with luxury perfumes and inspired fragrances alike. The problem is almost never the scent itself.</p><p>Fragrance longevity is not down to luck. It comes down to a few things most people have never been shown. Here is what actually works.</p>"
      }
    },
    "177868816090d620d4": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
