    "blogtextliquid_ra7Awx": {
      "type": "blogtextliquid",
      "custom_css": [
        "@media screen and (min-width: 1024px) {.shopify-hardcoded-text-section-content {font-size: 24px !important; margin: 0 !important; } .shopify-hardcoded-text-section-wrapper {margin-bottom: -85px; }}",
        ".shopify-hardcoded-text-section-content {font-size: 16px; margin-left: 0px; text-align: left;}",
        "@media screen and (max-width: 1024px) {.shopify-hardcoded-text-section-wrapper {min-height: 200px; padding: 16px !important; }}"
      ],
      "name": "Blog Text Liquid",
      "settings": {
        "text_content": "<p>Dry skin is the biggest culprit. Scent needs something to hold onto, and under-hydrated skin is basically a non-stick surface for fragrance. This is why the same perfume can smell completely different on two people and fade at totally different speeds. Oily skin holds scent far longer. That is not a flaw in the fragrance or in you. It is just chemistry, and chemistry does not care how much you paid for the bottle.</p><p>Application is the other piece. Most people spray once and walk out the door, which means the fragrance blooms briefly and checks out before the morning is over. The right spots make a real difference, and most people are not using them.</p><p>And then there is concentration, which barely anyone talks about, and where a lot of money gets wasted on something that was never going to make it past mid-morning.</p>"
      }
    },
